[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      7[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## What the C program actually does
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main` is straight-line code with no data-dependent branches:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m printLine("Calling good()...");  good();  printLine("Finished good()");
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m printLine("Calling bad()...");   bad();   printLine("Finished bad()");
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m return 0;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m It never reads stdin, never inspects `argc`/`argv`, and reads no environment
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m variables. Its output is a fixed 92 bytes and its exit status is always 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m So the input space that can change behavior is not the argument vector or
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdin — it is **the state of the standard file descriptors**, which is where
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m both real mismatches were found.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Branch inventory, and how each is reached:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Location | Branch | Reachable? | Covered by |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `printLine` | `line != NULL` true | yes, all 7 calls | every test |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `printLine` | `line == NULL` false-branch (no output) | **no** — every caller passes a string literal | structurally unreachable; preserved as `Option::None` in the translation |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `helperBad` | whole function | **no** — `static`, never called | `helper_bad_is_never_called` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `helperGood` | whole function | yes, via `good()` | every test |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `main` | — | no branches | — |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatch 1 — broken stdout pipe: C died from SIGPIPE, Rust exited 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** With stdout on a pipe whose read end was already closed:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | | exit status |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C | killed by signal 13 (`SIGPIPE`); shell reports 141 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust (before fix) | exited 0 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdout and stderr agreed (both empty); only the status differed. A test that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m checked stdout alone would have passed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** Two things compounding:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. Rust's standard library sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main` runs. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    C program inherits the default disposition. So the failing write raised no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    signal in the Rust program and returned `EPIPE` instead.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. The translation discarded that error with `let _ = out.write_all(...)`, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    the `EPIPE` went nowhere and `main` fell through to `exit(0)`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `reset_sigpipe()` in `src/main.rs` restores `SIG_DFL` for `SIGPIPE` at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the top of `main`, via a direct `extern "C"` declaration of `signal` (no new
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m dependency for one symbol). Discarding the write error is *correct* and was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m kept — see mismatch 3 — the defect was purely the signal disposition.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Regression-checked.** Commenting out `reset_sigpipe()` makes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `broken_stdout_pipe_matches` fail and leaves the other 28 tests passing, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m test is genuinely load-bearing.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatch 2 — stdout buffering discipline (latent, not observable here)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** glibc gives `stdout` *full* buffering when it is not a terminal and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m *line* buffering when it is. `std::io::Stdout` is always a `LineWriter`. The C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program therefore emits its entire output in a single 92-byte write at exit when
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdout is a pipe or file, whereas the original translation emitted six separate
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m writes.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** The `Out` writer in `src/main.rs` reproduces glibc's rule: it checks
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `isatty(1)`, flushing per newline on a terminal and otherwise buffering
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m everything until the exit-time flush.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Honest scope.** No deterministic test in this suite distinguishes the two
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m disciplines, and I verified that: forcing `line_buffered: true` unconditionally
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m still leaves all 29 tests green. The reason is that the total output (92 bytes)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is far smaller than any pipe buffer, so the final byte sequence is identical
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m either way, and the write *count* is only observable to a reader that consumes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m part of the stream and then closes — which is inherently racy to assert. In the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m broken-pipe test the reader is gone before the first write, so both disciplines
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m deliver zero bytes and die identically. This change is recorded as fidelity to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the C's I/O model rather than as a fix for an observed diff.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Non-mismatches — behaviors confirmed identical, and deliberately not "fixed"
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`helperBad()` is dead code.** The C defines it `static` and never calls it,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   so `bad()` prints only `bad()`. This looks like a bug and was left exactly as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   is; `helper_bad_is_never_called` asserts the string never appears in either
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   program's output, so a future "fix" would fail the suite. The Rust keeps the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   function with `#[allow(dead_code)]` to mirror the source.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`printLine`'s NULL guard is unreachable.** No input can drive it. It is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   preserved as `Option<&str>` so the structure still matches the C.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Write errors do not change the exit status.** With stdout on `/dev/full`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (ENOSPC) or with fd 1 closed outright (EBADF), the C still exits 0, because
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   it discards `printf`'s return value and glibc's exit-time flush failure does
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   not alter the status. Verified for both, so the `let _ =` discards in the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   translation are faithful, not sloppy.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **stdin is never read.** Empty, one line, no trailing newline, 10 000 lines,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   4 KiB of binary including NUL bytes, invalid UTF-8, whitespace only, and fd 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   closed all produce identical output and status.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`argc`/`argv` are ignored.** No args, one arg, an empty-string arg, args
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   containing newlines and non-ASCII UTF-8, a 100 000-character arg, 2 000 args,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   a rewritten `argv[0]`, and an empty `argv[0]` all agree.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Locale is irrelevant.** `%s` on ASCII literals is locale independent;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   checked under `C`, `C.UTF-8`, `en_US.UTF-8`, `tr_TR.UTF-8` and an invalid
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   locale name.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **stdout on a terminal.** Under a pty (via `script`) both emit the same bytes,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   including the pty's LF→CRLF translation.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Determinism.** Five consecutive runs of each are byte-identical.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **stderr is never written** by either program in any case tested.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
